<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vectors-parallel-to-line" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Creating a vector parallel to a line in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	create a vector that is parallel to the given line.
	Then draw both the graph of the line and your vector with its initial point at the origin.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \ell \colon \;\; x - 3 y = 4 </m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p> One such vector is <m> \uvec{v} = (3,1) </m>. </p>

		<image width="60.5%">
			<latex-image label="image-vectors-exercises-parallel-to-line-answer-1">
				<xi:include href="parallel-to-line.d/answer-1.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram of a line and a vector parallel to it. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of the graph of the line <m> x - 3 y = 4 </m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				along with the vector <m> \uvec{v} = (3,1) </m> drawn with its initial point at the origin,
				so that its terminal point is at <m> (3,1) </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

		<p> <em>Note:</em> Any nonzero scalar multiple of the provided answer is another suitable answer. </p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\ell \colon \;\; 2 x + 5 y = 5</m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p> One such vector is <m> \uvec{v} = (5,-2) </m>. </p>

		<image width="64.7%">
			<latex-image label="image-vectors-exercises-parallel-to-line-answer-2">
				<xi:include href="parallel-to-line.d/answer-2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram of a line and a vector parallel to it. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of the graph of the line <m> 2 x + 5 y = 5 </m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				along with the vector <m> \uvec{v} = (5,-2) </m> drawn with its initial point at the origin,
				so that its terminal point is at <m> (5,-2) </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

		<p> <em>Note:</em> Any nonzero scalar multiple of the provided answer is another suitable answer. </p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
